1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,301
Hi, Rob.

2
00:00:01,301 --> 00:00:02,480
How's the course going?

3
00:00:02,480 --> 00:00:03,615
Oh, hi, Mia.

4
00:00:03,615 --> 00:00:04,372
Yeah, great.

5
00:00:04,372 --> 00:00:07,400
I can't believe the first term's nearly over.

6
00:00:07,400 --> 00:00:12,075
I saw your group's performance last night at the student theatre.

7
00:00:12,075 --> 00:00:13,350
It was good.

8
00:00:13,350 --> 00:00:13,687
Really?

9
00:00:13,687 --> 00:00:14,024
Yeah ...

10
00:00:14,024 --> 00:00:18,741
but now we have to write a report on the whole thing, an in-depth

11
00:00:18,741 --> 00:00:19,078
analysis.

12
00:00:19,078 --> 00:00:21,100
I don't know where to start.

13
00:00:21,100 --> 00:00:25,226
Like I have to write about the role I played, the doctor, how I

14
00:00:25,226 --> 00:00:26,110
developed the character.

15
00:00:26,110 --> 00:00:28,540
Well, what was your starting point?

16
00:00:28,540 --> 00:00:28,912
Er...

17
00:00:28,912 --> 00:00:31,892
my grandfather was a doctor before he retired,

18
00:00:31,892 --> 00:00:34,500
and I just based it on him.

19
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:34,872
OK,

20
00:00:34,872 --> 00:00:35,616
but how?

21
00:00:35,616 --> 00:00:38,220
Did you talk to him about it?

22
00:00:38,220 --> 00:00:40,711
He must have all sorts of stories,

23
00:00:40,711 --> 00:00:44,269
but he never says much about his work, even now.

24
00:00:44,269 --> 00:00:46,760
He has a sort of authority though.

25
00:00:46,760 --> 00:00:50,780
So how did you manage to capture that?

26
00:00:50,780 --> 00:00:51,133
I'd ...

27
00:00:51,133 --> 00:00:55,020
I'd visualise what he must have been like in the past,

28
00:00:55,020 --> 00:00:59,260
when he was sitting in his consulting room listening to his patients.

29
00:00:59,260 --> 00:00:59,692
OK,

30
00:00:59,692 --> 00:01:03,150
so that's what you explain in your report.

31
00:01:03,150 --> 00:01:04,030
Right.

32
00:01:04,030 --> 00:01:08,772
Then there's the issue of atmosphere - so in the first scene we needed

33
00:01:08,772 --> 00:01:13,515
to know how boring life was in the doctor's village in the 1950s,

34
00:01:13,515 --> 00:01:18,622
so when the curtain went up on the first scene in the waiting room,

35
00:01:18,622 --> 00:01:21,540
there was that long silence before anyone spoke.

36
00:01:21,540 --> 00:01:27,168
And then people kept saving the same thing over and over, like 'Cold, isn't

37
00:01:27,168 --> 00:01:27,570
it?'

38
00:01:27,570 --> 00:01:27,934
Yes,

39
00:01:27,934 --> 00:01:30,117
and everyone wore grey and brown,

40
00:01:30,117 --> 00:01:32,300
and just sat in a row.

41
00:01:32,300 --> 00:01:35,120
Yes, all those details of the production.

42
00:01:35,120 --> 00:01:39,258
And I have to analyse how I functioned in the group - what I

43
00:01:39,258 --> 00:01:40,531
found out about myself.

44
00:01:40,531 --> 00:01:43,077
I know I was so frustrated at times,

45
00:01:43,077 --> 00:01:44,350
when we couldn't agree.

46
00:01:44,350 --> 00:01:44,837
Yes.

47
00:01:44,837 --> 00:01:48,730
So did one person emerge as the leader?

48
00:01:48,730 --> 00:01:49,382
Sophia did.

49
00:01:49,382 --> 00:01:53,292
That was OK, she helped us work out exactly what to do.

50
00:01:53,292 --> 00:01:53,943
forthe production.

51
00:01:53,943 --> 00:01:55,898
And that made me feel better.

52
00:01:55,898 --> 00:01:56,550
I suppose.

53
00:01:56,550 --> 00:01:59,090
When you understood what needed doing?

54
00:01:59,090 --> 00:01:59,506
Yes.

55
00:01:59,506 --> 00:02:02,005
And Sophia did some research, too.

56
00:02:02,005 --> 00:02:04,920
That was useful in developing our approach.

57
00:02:04,920 --> 00:02:05,930
Like what?

58
00:02:05,930 --> 00:02:10,502
Well, she found these articles from the 1950s about how relationships between children

59
00:02:10,502 --> 00:02:11,557
and their parents,

60
00:02:11,557 --> 00:02:16,480
or between the public and people like bank managers or the police were shifting.

61
00:02:16,480 --> 00:02:16,988
Interesting.

62
00:02:16,988 --> 00:02:21,560
And did you have any practical problems to overcome?

63
00:02:21,560 --> 00:02:25,860
Well, in the final rehearsal everything was going fine until the last scene -

64
00:02:25,860 --> 00:02:29,830
that's where the doctor's first patient appears on stage on his own.

65
00:02:29,830 --> 00:02:31,320
The one in the wheelchair?

66
00:02:31,320 --> 00:02:31,640
Yes,

67
00:02:31,640 --> 00:02:36,768
and he had this really long speech, with the stage all dark except for one spotlight -

68
00:02:36,768 --> 00:02:41,255
and then that stuck somehow so it was shining on the wrong side of

69
00:02:41,255 --> 00:02:41,896
the stage ...

70
00:02:41,896 --> 00:02:44,460
but anyway we got that fixed, thank goodness.

71
00:02:44,460 --> 00:02:48,890
Yes, it was fine on the night.

72
00:02:48,890 --> 00:02:54,434
But while you're here, Mia, I wanted to ask you about the year abroad

73
00:02:54,434 --> 00:02:54,830
option.

74
00:02:54,830 --> 00:02:56,810
Would you recommend doing that?

75
00:02:56,810 --> 00:02:57,623
Yes, definitely.

76
00:02:57,623 --> 00:03:02,500
It's a fantastic chance to study in another country for a year.

77
00:03:02,500 --> 00:03:04,703
I think I'd like to do it,

78
00:03:04,703 --> 00:03:08,480
but it looks very competitive - there's only a limited number of places.

79
00:03:08,480 --> 00:03:08,822
Yes,

80
00:03:08,822 --> 00:03:13,603
so next year when you are in the second year of the course, you

81
00:03:13,603 --> 00:03:17,360
need to work really hard in all your theatre studies modules.

82
00:03:17,360 --> 00:03:22,242
Only students with good marks get places - you have to prove that you

83
00:03:22,242 --> 00:03:24,120
know your subject really well.

84
00:03:24,120 --> 00:03:24,486
Right.

85
00:03:24,486 --> 00:03:27,410
So how did you choose where to go?

86
00:03:27,410 --> 00:03:32,960
Well.I decided I wanted a programme that would fit in with what I wanted

87
00:03:32,960 --> 00:03:34,810
to do after I graduate,

88
00:03:34,810 --> 00:03:39,990
so I looked for a university with emphasis on acting rather than directing for

89
00:03:39,990 --> 00:03:40,360
example.

90
00:03:40,360 --> 00:03:46,817
It depends on you.Then about six months before you go, you have to email

91
00:03:46,817 --> 00:03:50,260
the scheme coordinator with your top three choices.

92
00:03:50,260 --> 00:03:54,288
I had a friend who missed the deadline and didn't get her first choice,

93
00:03:54,288 --> 00:03:57,740
so you do need to get a move on at that stage.

94
00:03:57,740 --> 00:04:01,280
You'll find that certain places are very popular with everyone.

95
00:04:01,280 --> 00:04:04,690
And don't you have to write a personal statement at that stage?

96
00:04:04,690 --> 00:04:05,230
Yes.

97
00:04:05,230 --> 00:04:09,065
Right.I'll get some of the final year students to give me some tips, maybe

98
00:04:09,065 --> 00:04:11,110
see if I can read what they wrote.

99
00:04:11,110 --> 00:04:13,034
I thinkthat's a very good idea.

100
00:04:13,034 --> 00:04:15,600
I don't mind showing you what I did.

101
00:04:15,600 --> 00:04:19,440
And while you're abroad don't make the mistake I made.

102
00:04:19,440 --> 00:04:23,696
I got so involved I forgot all about making arrangements for when I came

103
00:04:23,696 --> 00:04:25,520
back here for the final year.

104
00:04:25,520 --> 00:04:30,392
Make sure you stay in touch so they know your choices for the optional

105
00:04:30,392 --> 00:04:30,740
modules.

106
00:04:30,740 --> 00:04:34,410
You don't want to miss out doing your preferred specialisms.

107
00:04:34,410 --> 00:04:35,270
Right.
